## ⚠️ THE BIG RESULT — 2026-08-24: the model loses to the market

Source: `kalshi_snapshots` joined to `settlements`. 15,027 snapshot rows,
72 days, 20 cities, Jun 13 – Aug 23. This table was sitting unused. It holds the
market's full 6-bracket ladder at both windows plus `model_top_bracket`, so the
model's pick can be scored against the market's pick against actual settlement.

### 1. When model and market disagree, the market wins 762 to 171

On 1,269 disagreements where either side was right:

| outcome | n |
|---|---|
| MARKET right | 762 |
| both wrong | 336 |
| MODEL right | 171 |

**81.7% of decided disagreements go to the market.** The punchlist's own
manual-grid note said "market is right ~75-80% when model disagrees" — this is
worse, on a much larger automated sample. **This is the same monotonic-inverse
pattern that killed the tennis model.** It is not a small-sample artifact.

### 2. Model AGREEMENT is anti-predictive

Splitting the market's rank-1 bracket by whether the model agreed with it:

| window / band | AGREE hit / PnL¢ | DISAGREE hit / PnL¢ |
|---|---|---|
| CONV 45-59 | 36.6% / **−16.12** | 52.0% / −0.20 |
| CONV 60-69 | 56.3% / **−7.83** | 69.8% / **+5.94** |
| EDGE 45-59 | 42.5% / −9.76 | 52.6% / +0.70 |
| EDGE 60-69 | 61.8% / −1.56 | 72.9% / **+9.10** |

In 7 of 8 cells the market's favorite performs WORSE when the model agrees with
it. At ~64¢ agreement drops the hit rate from 69.8% to 56.3%. **The model's
endorsement is currently a negative signal.**

**The one exception:** `EDGE / AGREE / 70+` → 86.7% hit at 75.4¢ avg, **+11.30¢**,
n=30. This is the July "34-for-34 on 70¢+" finding surviving into an independent
dataset. It is the only agreement cell that pays. n=30 over 72 days is roughly one
qualifying bet every other day and is below the 50-bet discipline bar.

Label formats were verified to match (`NN-NN` on both sides) before trusting this
split — this is not a string-matching artifact.

### 3. THE MECHANISM — bracket selection is cold, the forecast is not

Model's chosen bracket floor vs actual, and market's bracket floor vs actual:

| city | model_vs_actual | market_vs_actual | consensus_vs_actual |
|---|---|---|---|
| Atlanta | −3.03 | −0.73 | −0.75 |
| Denver | −2.74 | −0.78 | −0.41 |
| Washington DC | −2.62 | −0.24 | −0.72 |
| Phoenix | −2.25 | −0.59 | −0.40 |
| Austin | −2.22 | −0.51 | −0.45 |
| Las Vegas | −2.13 | −0.71 | −1.20 |
| Dallas | +0.04 | −0.42 | +0.24 |

**The consensus forecast is roughly centered** (mixed sign, mostly within ±0.8°F).
**The market is nearly unbiased** (−0.09 to −0.78, avg ~−0.44, well inside a 2°F
bracket). **But the model's chosen bracket is 1–3°F cold.**

Atlanta: consensus off by −0.75°F, chosen bracket off by −3.03°F.
Denver: consensus −0.41, bracket −2.74. DC: −0.72 vs −2.62.

**A ~0.5°F consensus error is being amplified into a 1–3°F bracket error.** The
leak is in the ladder/probability placement BETWEEN consensus and bracket choice —
not in the forecast, and not (only) in gate permissiveness as the July analysis
concluded. Trimmed mean does not address this.

The model is cold in 19 of 20 cities (only Dallas and SF are not). The offset is
NOT uniform — measured against the market it ranges from −0.16 (New York) to
−2.36 (Washington DC), so any correction is per-city, not global.

### 4. Market-level price bands (rank-1 bracket, ignoring model)

Actual P&L per contract at real prices, not band midpoints:

| band | n | avg price | CONV PnL¢ | EDGE PnL¢ |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| <45 | 255 | ~41¢ | **+7.91** | **+3.32** |
| 45-59 | 1229 | ~52¢ | **−5.47** | −2.56 |
| 60-69 | 450 | ~64¢ | +1.13 | **+5.49** |
| 70+ | 250 | ~76¢ | −3.14 | +0.43 |

The 45-59¢ poison band is **independently confirmed** on 1,229 observations —
this reproduces the July finding on a completely different dataset. The 60¢ floor
correctly excludes it.

**CAVEAT — this is the MARKET's top bracket, not the model's pick.** It describes
market structure. It does NOT say the model would profit in these bands. See the
price-floor section below.

### 5. Market microstructure: Edge → Conviction drift

The favorite gains price between windows in **all 20 cities** without exception —
the day resolves. But the useful column is bracket-identity FLIP rate:

- **Las Vegas 8.8% flips**, highest Edge price (66.3¢) — settled early, no
  waiting premium and no discount.
- **Los Angeles 35.3% flips, +11.9¢ drift** — the outlier. Favorite identity
  changes a third of the time and then resolves harder than anywhere (72.2¢ at
  Conviction). Strongest Conviction city, worst Edge city.
- **Denver 23.7%, Houston 23.1%, Boston 23.0%, Dallas 22.7%** — churning late.
  Early entry buys a bracket that changes identity ~1 day in 4.
- **Atlanta (14.8%) and Washington DC (18.0%)** are the interesting cell: low
  flip rate, CHEAPEST Edge prices on the board (50.8¢, 49.6¢), near-zero drift.
  Market prices them as uncertain and they aren't actually moving.

Flip rate measures whether the MARKET changed its mind, not whether it was right.

