### ⚠️ THE SPEC'S CENTRAL PREMISE IS FALSE — timing cannot anchor the model

`lows_timing_probe.py`, 31 days × 18 cities of `low_time_utc` converted to local:

- **Only 3/18 cities have tight timing** (spread ≤3h): Dallas (1.7h), Houston
  (2.4h), New Orleans (2.7h).
- **9/18 have p10-p90 spreads over 6h.** Phoenix is **23.0h** — its minimum has
  landed at essentially every hour of the day.
- **8/18 put the minimum OUTSIDE the 03:00-09:00 dawn window on ≥20% of days.**
  Miami does it **48%** of the time.
- Median timing IS stable everywhere (05:36 to 07:30 across all 18). The
  distribution has a tight center and **fat tails**.

So "the observed minimum is a CEILING that locks at sunrise" — the premise the
whole certainty-curve play rests on — **is false for most cities.** There is no
reliable locked moment. A timing-gated lows model would be wrong about half the
time in the worst cases.

Confirmed independently in the intraday collector: on Aug 23 Miami's `temp_2m`
bottomed near 19:00 local at 74.7°F, ~5°F below anything the night produced,
after an afternoon convective collapse. Miami was the ONLY city of 18 where the
evening minimum undercut the overnight minimum that day — but the 31-day timing
data says it happens there 48% of the time. Not a one-off storm.

### THE AXIS THAT WORKS: value dispersion, not timing

`lows_value_probe.py`, same 31 days, against the 2°F Kalshi bracket width:

| city | stdev | d/d abs delta | ≤2F next day | brackets spanned |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Los Angeles | 1.06 | 0.67 | **100%** | 1.5 |
| San Antonio | 1.29 | 1.07 | 93% | 2.5 |
| Houston | 1.46 | 1.47 | 80% | 4.0 |
| New Orleans | 1.62 | 1.30 | 93% | 3.0 |
| Atlanta | 2.09 | 2.03 | 63% | 4.5 |
| Dallas | 2.35 | 1.73 | 77% | 6.0 |
| Miami | 2.66 | 2.53 | 57% | 5.5 |
| Denver | 5.51 | 4.30 | 23% | 13.5 |
| Las Vegas | 5.90 | 2.00 | 70% | 11.5 |

**Timing spread and diurnal range are INVERSELY related** — tight-timing cities
have big ranges (Dallas 20.9°F, OKC 27.4°F, Denver 31.2°F), loose-timing cities
are compressed (LA 11.1°F, Miami 14.1°F, Boston 14.1°F). Physically sensible: a
large diurnal swing has a sharp bottom; a compressed one is flat and any hour can
win by a tenth of a degree.

**Los Angeles proves the point.** Timing spread 6.8h, late-min 29% of days — the
timing premise would have EXCLUDED it. Its value stdev is 1.06°F with 100%
day-over-day persistence. It is the most predictable city on the board.

**→ The lows model should gate on VALUE, not on the clock.** No certainty curve,
no observed-min tracker, no cron window tuned to sunrise.

**BUT — predictable ≠ profitable.** A city whose low sits in 1.5 brackets all
month will have that bracket priced at 90¢+, which is the same trap already found
on the highs side. The interesting band is the MIDDLE (Atlanta 2.09, Dallas 2.35,
Austin 2.46, Miami 2.66): dispersed enough that the market cannot just price
persistence, tight enough that a forecast might beat it. **This is unresolved —
the price probe has not yet run against live markets.**

